  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming is defined as a politician taking personal, team, or delegation responsibility for producing a specific, desirable government outcome or localized benefit (such as securing federal grants, passing a specific piece of legislation, including a sponsored amendment, or resolving constituency casework).",
    "clarification": "To qualify as credit-claiming, the senator must explicitly link their actions (e.g., authoring a bill, introducing an amendment, writing letters, inserting appropriations, or direct personal advocacy) to a completed, positive outcome or benefit. Typical outcomes include federal grants awarded to local entities, the passage of a bill or amendment, and specific casework interventions. Simply introducing a bill, requesting aid, or co-sponsoring pending legislation without an established outcome is position-taking or advertising, not credit-claiming.",
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "document_id": "2697",
        "explanation": "Senator Inouye explicitly claims personal credit for bypassing a denied federal request and directly identifying and securing an alternative federal funding source ($225,000) to combat coqui frogs on the Big Island."
